/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · literature batch y */
WB.L("dictionary-makers", "literature", "beginner", "The people who wrote the dictionary", "Somebody had to decide what every word means. Meet the stubborn, eccentric and occasionally imprisoned people who built the English dictionary.",
  [
    ["Before dictionaries", "For most of its history, English had no dictionary at all.", "Picture Shakespeare writing his plays without a single dictionary on his desk. That was normal. For centuries, English speakers simply used words and argued about them. The first English dictionaries appeared around 1600 and were modest things. Robert Cawdrey's A Table Alphabeticall, published in 1604, listed only about 2,500 'hard words', mostly borrowed from Latin, French and Greek, with brief explanations for readers who found them puzzling. Nobody thought ordinary words like 'dog' or 'run' needed defining, because everyone already knew them. These early books were more like glossaries for the newly educated than records of the whole language. The idea that a dictionary should capture every word, common or rare, and show how each one was really used took another century and a half to arrive."],
    ["Samuel Johnson takes on the job", "Johnson produced a landmark dictionary almost single-handedly in about nine years.", "In 1746 a group of London booksellers hired Samuel Johnson, a brilliant but often broke writer, to produce a proper dictionary of English. The French Academy had taken decades to make its national dictionary with dozens of scholars. Johnson, working with a handful of copying assistants in a house off Fleet Street, took about nine years. His Dictionary of the English Language appeared in 1755 with roughly 42,000 entries. Its real innovation was evidence. Johnson read widely through English writers and marked passages that showed words in action, then used more than 100,000 quotations to illustrate meanings. That method, defining words by how good writers actually used them, became the foundation of serious dictionary making in English for centuries afterwards."],
    ["Johnson's sense of humour", "A few famous entries reveal Johnson's wit and his personality.", "Johnson's dictionary is mostly sober and careful, but a few entries let his personality peek through. He defined 'lexicographer' as 'a writer of dictionaries; a harmless drudge', poking fun at himself. His definition of 'oats' is the most quoted of all: 'a grain, which in England is generally given to horses, but in Scotland supports the people.' Johnson enjoyed teasing the Scots, even though his close friend and biographer James Boswell was one. These jokes are rare, and modern readers sometimes overrate them, but they help explain why the book became so loved. It was the work of a real person with opinions, not an anonymous committee. That human voice is part of why Johnson is still one of the most quoted figures in English."],
    ["Noah Webster's American project", "Webster used his dictionary to give the United States its own spelling.", "Across the Atlantic, the schoolteacher Noah Webster believed the new United States deserved its own version of English. He published An American Dictionary of the English Language in 1828, and he used it to push spelling changes he thought were simpler and more logical. That is why Americans write 'color', 'center' and 'defense' while Australians, following British habits, write 'colour', 'centre' and 'defence'. Webster wanted even bigger reforms, but most of his more radical ideas never caught on. Still, his choices stuck where it mattered most: in schools, newspapers and printing houses. A single determined dictionary maker helped split English spelling into two traditions, and every spellchecker that asks whether you want Australian or American English is living with his legacy."],
    ["The Oxford English Dictionary", "The OED set out to trace the history of every English word, and it took about 70 years.", "In 1857 members of the Philological Society in London called for a new kind of dictionary, one that would record every English word since around the 12th century and show how its meaning had changed over time. The project grew far beyond anyone's expectations. A Scottish schoolmaster named James Murray became editor in 1879 and worked in a corrugated iron shed he called the Scriptorium, lined with pigeonholes for slips of paper. The first part appeared in 1884, covering only 'A' to 'Ant'. Murray died in 1915, long before the end. The complete dictionary was finally finished in 1928, in ten large volumes, roughly 70 years after it was first proposed."],
    ["An army of volunteers", "Ordinary readers, including one remarkable prisoner, sent in millions of quotations.", "The OED could never have been written by its editors alone. Murray issued public appeals asking readers to search books for examples of words and post them in on slips of paper. Thousands of volunteers in Britain, America, Australia and elsewhere responded, and millions of slips arrived over the years. One of the most productive contributors was William Chester Minor, an American former army surgeon who was confined to Broadmoor, a hospital for the criminally insane in England, after killing a man in London in 1872. From his room, surrounded by books, Minor sent thousands of carefully chosen quotations. His story was later told in a popular book and film. It is a striking reminder that dictionaries are built by communities of readers, not only by scholars."],
    ["Dictionaries today", "Modern dictionaries describe how people really speak rather than lay down the law.", "Australia got its own major dictionary, the Macquarie, in 1981, recording words like 'arvo', 'doona' and 'ute' alongside Australian meanings and pronunciations. That shift reflects a bigger change in thinking. Early dictionary makers often wanted to fix the language and decide what was correct. Most modern lexicographers are descriptive instead: they observe how people actually use words and record it, including slang and new coinages. When a dictionary adds a word, it is not approving it, only noting that enough people use it. Today editors search huge digital collections of text to spot new words and shifting meanings, and online dictionaries are updated several times a year. The job Johnson did with paper slips and quill pens now runs on databases, but the goal is much the same."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did early English dictionaries like Cawdrey's mainly list?", ["Everyday words like 'dog' and 'run'", "Hard words borrowed from other languages", "Place names", "Slang"], 1, "Early dictionaries were glossaries of difficult, mostly borrowed words."],
    [1, "What was the key innovation in Johnson's 1755 dictionary?", ["Colour illustrations", "Pronunciation symbols", "Quotations showing words in use", "Alphabetical order"], 2, "Johnson used more than 100,000 quotations from writers to illustrate meanings."],
    [3, "Why do Americans write 'color' instead of 'colour'?", ["A law passed by Congress", "Noah Webster's dictionary", "Typewriter keyboards", "Spanish influence"], 1, "Webster's 1828 dictionary promoted simplified American spellings that stuck."],
    [4, "Roughly how long did the Oxford English Dictionary take to complete?", ["About 70 years", "About 10 years", "About 25 years", "About 150 years"], 0, "Proposed in 1857, it was finished in 1928."],
    [6, "What does it mean that modern dictionaries are 'descriptive'?", ["They only include formal words", "They tell you which words are wrong", "They record how people actually use language", "They describe words with pictures"], 2, "Descriptive dictionaries observe and record real usage rather than dictate it."]
  ]);

WB.L("australian-english", "literature", "beginner", "How Australian English was born", "Within a generation of 1788, visitors noticed that children in the colony sounded different. Here is how a new way of speaking English took shape.",
  [
    ["A new accent in a new place", "The Australian accent formed surprisingly quickly among the first colonial children.", "The First Fleet that arrived in 1788 carried people from all over Britain and Ireland: Londoners, people from rural England, Irish convicts, Scots and more. Their parents spoke a jumble of regional dialects. But children growing up together in Sydney did what children everywhere do: they copied each other more than their parents. By the 1820s, visitors were already remarking that native born children, sometimes called currency lads and lasses, had a distinct way of speaking. Linguists call this dialect levelling. When many accents are mixed together, the strongest regional quirks tend to drop out, and a new shared accent emerges. That is a big part of why Australian English came out fairly uniform across a huge continent from very early on."],
    ["Words from Aboriginal languages", "Many everyday Australian words were borrowed from Aboriginal languages.", "When Captain Cook's expedition was stranded near what is now Cooktown in 1770, his crew recorded a word that sounded like 'kangaroo' from the local Guugu Yimithirr people. It became one of the most famous loanwords in the world. After 1788, settlers in Sydney borrowed many more words from the Dharug language, including 'dingo', 'wombat' and 'koala'. 'Boomerang' also came from a Sydney area language, and 'billabong' is thought to come from Wiradjuri. Hundreds of Australian place names, from Wagga Wagga to Kakadu, have Aboriginal origins too. Before colonisation there were around 250 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages, many with several dialects. Many are now endangered, and communities around the country are working hard to revive and teach them."],
    ["Arvo, servo and brekkie", "Australians love shortening words and adding an 'o' or 'ie' to the end.", "Ask an Aussie what they did this arvo and you might hear that they stopped at the servo, grabbed a sanga and caught up with a rello. Australian English is famous for its diminutives: shortened words with an ending added. The two big endings are 'ie' or 'y', as in 'brekkie', 'barbie' and 'sunnies', and 'o', as in 'arvo', 'ambo', 'smoko' and 'bottle-o'. Linguists have counted thousands of these forms. Despite the stereotype, they are not about laziness. Research suggests they often signal friendliness, informality and shared identity, a way of saying 'we are relaxed with each other'. Notice how 'ambo' sounds warmer than 'paramedic'. Several of these words, like 'barbie', have travelled overseas, partly thanks to Australian films and television."],
    ["Fair dinkum and other mysteries", "Many classic Australian expressions have murky or surprising origins.", "Some of the most Australian sounding phrases turn out to have roots in British dialects. 'Dinkum', for example, appears in English regional speech in the 1800s, where it seems to have meant work or a fair share of work. In Australia it took on the sense of genuine or honest, and 'fair dinkum' became a national catchphrase. A popular story claims it comes from Chinese miners on the goldfields saying 'ding kum' for real gold, but there is no good evidence for this, and the British dialect origin is more likely. 'Cobber', meaning mate, may come from a British dialect word too. Etymology is full of tempting stories like these, and the less exciting explanation is usually the correct one."],
    ["Broad, general and cultivated", "Australian accents vary along a spectrum more than by region.", "In the 1960s, the linguists Alexander Mitchell and Arthur Delbridge recorded thousands of school students and described three broad types of Australian accent. 'Broad' is the stereotypical outback sound, heard in the voice of Steve Irwin. 'Cultivated' is closer to British pronunciation and was once common among radio announcers. 'General', in between, is what most Australians speak. These are points on a scale rather than separate boxes, and the same person may slide along it depending on the situation. Over recent decades, the cultivated accent has become much rarer, and Australians have grown more comfortable sounding Australian on the national broadcaster and in parliament. Regional differences exist, but they are small compared with Britain or the United States."],
    ["Bathers, togs or swimmers?", "Small regional word differences reveal where an Australian grew up.", "Australian accents may be fairly uniform, but vocabulary is a different story. What you wear to the beach depends on your state: 'bathers' is common in Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia, 'togs' in Queensland, and 'swimmers' or 'cossie' in New South Wales. A slice of fried potato in batter is a 'potato cake' in Melbourne and a 'potato scallop' in Sydney. The processed meat in school sandwiches has gone by names like 'devon', 'fritz', 'polony' and 'Belgium sausage', depending on where you lived. A 'port' is a school bag in parts of Queensland. These words travel less than accents do, so they work like little badges of home, and they make a fun conversation starter with anyone from interstate."],
    ["Not mumbling to keep out the flies", "The Australian accent is a normal accent with a normal history, not a lazy one.", "An old myth says Australians speak with half closed mouths to keep out the flies, or that the accent comes from convicts being drunk or lazy. Linguists reject these ideas. Every accent is simply the result of how a community's speech has drifted over time, and Australian English follows the same rules of sound change as any other variety. In 1965, a humorous book called Let Stalk Strine, by the pen name Afferbeck Lauder, played with how Australian speech sounds to the ear, and 'Strine' became a jokey name for the accent. Today Australian English sits alongside British, American, Indian and other Englishes as one of the major national varieties, with its own dictionaries, its own spelling habits and a growing cultural influence."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What process helped create a fairly uniform Australian accent?", ["Dialect levelling", "Government language laws", "Radio broadcasting", "Spelling reform"], 0, "Mixing many regional accents caused strong quirks to drop out, forming a new shared accent."],
    [1, "Which Aboriginal language gave English the word 'kangaroo'?", ["Wiradjuri", "Dharug", "Guugu Yimithirr", "Noongar"], 2, "Cook's crew recorded it from Guugu Yimithirr speakers near Cooktown in 1770."],
    [2, "According to research, what do diminutives like 'ambo' often signal?", ["Laziness", "Friendliness and informality", "Formality", "Lack of education"], 1, "Diminutives often signal warmth, informality and shared identity."],
    [3, "What is the most likely origin of 'dinkum'?", ["Chinese miners on the goldfields", "A Dharug word", "A British dialect word", "An American cowboy term"], 2, "It appears in British regional dialects, and the goldfields story lacks evidence."],
    [5, "In Queensland, what are swimwear most commonly called?", ["Bathers", "Togs", "Cossies", "Swimmers"], 1, "'Togs' is the usual Queensland word."]
  ]);

WB.L("punctuation-history", "literature", "intermediate", "The secret history of punctuation", "Ancient Romans wrote without spaces, commas or full stops. The marks we now take for granted were invented one by one, often by monks and printers.",
  [
    ["Words without spaces", "Ancient Greek and Latin texts were usually written as one unbroken stream of letters.", "Imagine reading a book where every line looked like THISISHOWMOSTANCIENTTEXTSLOOKED. Much ancient Greek and Latin was written in this style, which scholars call scriptio continua, meaning continuous script. There were no spaces between words, no lower case letters and very little punctuation. It worked because reading was usually done aloud, often by trained readers who prepared a text in advance, much as an actor studies a script. The reader worked out where words and sentences broke by sounding them out. To us it seems maddening, but to a skilled Roman reader it was simply normal. Punctuation, as we know it, began as a set of helpful notes for performers, telling them where to pause and breathe."],
    ["Dots for breathing", "An ancient librarian invented dots at different heights to mark pauses.", "Around 200 BCE, a scholar named Aristophanes of Byzantium, who worked at the great library in Alexandria, is credited with a simple system to help readers. He placed dots at different heights beside the text. A low dot marked a short pause, a middle dot a slightly longer one, and a high dot the end of a complete thought. These pauses had names that still echo today. The short section was a 'komma', Greek for a piece cut off, and the complete thought was a 'periodos', a full circuit. Those words became our comma and period. His system was used patchily for centuries, and many writers ignored punctuation entirely, leaving it to readers to add their own marks."],
    ["Monks and the gift of spaces", "Irish and English scribes began separating words to help readers of Latin.", "The humble space between words may be the most important punctuation mark of all, and we owe it largely to early medieval monks. From around the 7th century, Irish and Anglo-Saxon scribes copying Latin texts began leaving gaps between words. Latin was a foreign language to them, so they could not rely on knowing it by ear, and spaces made it far easier to read. The practice spread across Europe over the following centuries. Word spacing also helped make silent reading more common, because readers no longer needed to sound out text to find where words began and ended. In monasteries, scribes also experimented with marks to show questions and sentence endings, slowly building the toolkit we use today."],
    ["The printers step in", "Venetian printers gave us the semicolon and helped standardise punctuation.", "When printing spread across Europe in the late 1400s, punctuation became more consistent, because thousands of copies now came from the same set of type. One of the most influential printers was Aldus Manutius in Venice. In the 1490s his press printed a work by the scholar Pietro Bembo that used the semicolon, a mark that became popular across Europe. Aldus is also associated with italic type and with the elegant small books that were the paperbacks of their day. Printers needed rules so that compositors setting type would make the same choices, and over time they settled on many of the conventions we still follow. Much of what we call correct punctuation began as a printing house's house style."],
    ["The apostrophe's troubled life", "The apostrophe arrived in English in the 1500s and has caused confusion ever since.", "The apostrophe came into English in the 16th century, borrowed from French and Italian printing, where it marked missing letters, as in 'don't' for 'do not'. Its use for possession, as in 'the dog's bone', developed gradually and was not fully settled until around the 1700s and 1800s. Even then, there were awkward exceptions, such as 'its' for possession and 'it's' for 'it is', which still trip up careful writers. The so called greengrocer's apostrophe, as in 'banana's $3 a kilo', is a much mocked error, but it shows how slippery the rules really are. Some place names have dropped the apostrophe altogether, and Australian naming authorities generally prefer place names without it, as in Kings Cross."],
    ["Inventions that failed", "Some new punctuation marks were proposed and never caught on.", "Not every new mark succeeds. In 1962, an American advertising executive named Martin Speckter proposed the interrobang, a question mark and exclamation mark merged into one symbol, for sentences like 'You did what?!' It had a brief burst of fame and even appeared on some typewriters, but it never entered everyday writing. Over the centuries others have suggested an irony mark, a backwards question mark to show a statement should not be taken literally, but none has stuck. The lesson is that punctuation spreads only when large numbers of writers, printers and readers adopt it at once. That is hard to arrange, which is why the core set of marks has changed surprisingly little in the past few hundred years."],
    ["Punctuation in the age of texting", "Online messaging is giving old marks new meanings.", "Send a friend a text that simply says 'Fine.' and they may worry you are upset. In casual messaging, the full stop at the end of a short message has picked up an extra meaning. Because chat often leaves sentences unpunctuated, adding a full stop can look deliberate, and some research suggests readers find such messages less sincere or a little cold. Exclamation marks, by contrast, often signal warmth rather than shouting. Ellipses can read as hesitant or passive aggressive, depending on who is reading. Emoji now do some of the work that tone of voice does in speech. This is not a decline in standards. It is punctuation doing what it has always done: adapting to help people make their meaning clear."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was 'scriptio continua'?", ["A style of handwriting with joined letters", "Writing with no spaces between words", "A medieval shorthand", "A Roman numbering system"], 1, "Many ancient texts ran all the words together without spaces."],
    [1, "The words 'comma' and 'period' come from which language?", ["Latin", "Greek", "Arabic", "French"], 1, "They come from the Greek 'komma' and 'periodos'."],
    [2, "Who began routinely putting spaces between words?", ["Roman senators", "Venetian printers", "Irish and Anglo-Saxon scribes", "Victorian schoolteachers"], 2, "Early medieval scribes in Ireland and England added spaces to help read Latin."],
    [3, "Which printer's press helped popularise the semicolon?", ["Aldus Manutius", "Johannes Gutenberg", "William Caxton", "Benjamin Franklin"], 0, "Aldus Manutius in Venice printed a 1490s work by Pietro Bembo using the semicolon."],
    [5, "What is an interrobang?", ["A backwards question mark", "A double full stop", "A question mark and exclamation mark combined", "An early form of the apostrophe"], 2, "Martin Speckter proposed it in 1962 for excited questions."]
  ]);

WB.L("detective-fiction", "literature", "beginner", "How the detective story was invented", "From a murder in a Paris apartment to Sherlock Holmes and Agatha Christie, the whodunnit has a surprisingly short and lively history.",
  [
    ["A locked room in Paris", "Edgar Allan Poe is usually credited with inventing the modern detective story in 1841.", "In 1841, the American writer Edgar Allan Poe published 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue'. Two women are found brutally killed in a Paris apartment that seems impossible to enter or leave. The police are baffled. Then an eccentric amateur named C. Auguste Dupin solves the mystery through pure reasoning, noticing clues everyone else missed. The answer is famously bizarre, and involves an escaped orangutan. Poe wrote only three Dupin stories, but they established the basic pattern of the genre: a brilliant, slightly odd detective, a less clever friend who narrates, a puzzling crime and a final explanation that makes everything click. Almost every fictional detective since owes something to Dupin."],
    ["Why then?", "Detective fiction emerged alongside modern police forces and growing cities.", "It is no accident that detective stories appeared in the 1800s. London's Metropolitan Police was founded in 1829, and in 1842 it set up a small detective branch. Big, crowded cities were full of strangers, and crime became something to be investigated with evidence rather than simply punished. Newspapers fed public fascination with real murder cases. At the same time, science was transforming how people thought about the world, and the idea that careful observation could reveal hidden truths was exciting. The fictional detective captured that spirit. He, and later she, was a kind of scientist of crime, bringing order and reason to the chaos of the modern city. Readers found that deeply reassuring, and many still do."],
    ["Enter Sherlock Holmes", "Arthur Conan Doyle's Holmes became the most famous detective in the world.", "In 1887, a young doctor named Arthur Conan Doyle published A Study in Scarlet, introducing Sherlock Holmes and his companion Dr Watson. Doyle modelled Holmes partly on Joseph Bell, a teacher at the Edinburgh medical school who could deduce a patient's job and history from small details. Holmes became a sensation when short stories appeared in The Strand Magazine from 1891. Doyle grew tired of him and in 1893 killed him off at the Reichenbach Falls in Switzerland. Readers were outraged, and many cancelled their magazine subscriptions. Doyle eventually gave in. Holmes returned, first in The Hound of the Baskervilles, set before his death, and then properly in 1903, when Doyle revealed he had survived the fall."],
    ["A Melbourne bestseller", "One of the 19th century's biggest crime novels was set in Melbourne.", "A year before Holmes first appeared, a young New Zealand raised lawyer named Fergus Hume published The Mystery of a Hansom Cab in Melbourne in 1886. It begins with a man found dead in a horse drawn cab travelling through the city at night, and it moves between the wealthy homes of Melbourne and the slums of Little Bourke Street. The book was a huge success in Australia and then in Britain, selling far more copies than Doyle's first Holmes novel and becoming one of the bestselling crime novels of its century. Hume reportedly sold the rights cheaply and saw little of the profit. Australia has kept up the tradition, from Arthur Upfield to Kerry Greenwood and Jane Harper."],
    ["The Golden Age", "Between the world wars, the puzzle mystery reached its peak.", "The years between the First and Second World Wars are often called the Golden Age of detective fiction. Agatha Christie published her first novel, The Mysterious Affair at Styles, in 1920, introducing the Belgian detective Hercule Poirot. She went on to become one of the bestselling novelists in history. Dorothy L. Sayers, Margery Allingham and New Zealand's Ngaio Marsh were among the other leading writers. These books were often set in country houses or villages, with a closed group of suspects and a body in the library. The fun was the puzzle itself. In 1930 many of these writers formed the Detection Club in London, a social club whose members promised to play fair with their readers."],
    ["Playing fair", "Golden Age writers believed readers deserved a fair chance to solve the crime.", "The central idea of the Golden Age was fair play. Every clue the detective used had to be shown to the reader too, so that a sharp enough reader could, in theory, solve the case first. In 1929 the writer and priest Ronald Knox published a tongue in cheek list of rules for detective fiction. They included that the criminal must be mentioned early in the story, that no supernatural forces should be involved, and that no undiscovered poisons should be used. Writers loved to test these rules. Christie broke expectations so cleverly in some books that readers felt cheated and delighted at the same time. The genre became a game between author and reader, with misdirection as the main weapon."],
    ["Mean streets and modern crime", "American hardboiled writers took the detective out of the drawing room.", "Not everyone loved tidy country house puzzles. In 1920s and 1930s America, writers like Dashiell Hammett and Raymond Chandler created hardboiled detectives: tough, weary private investigators working in corrupt cities like San Francisco and Los Angeles. Their stories were less about clever clues and more about violence, money and moral compromise, told in sharp, wisecracking prose. Chandler's Philip Marlowe became the model for countless film noir heroes. Since then, crime fiction has split into many branches: police procedurals, psychological thrillers, Scandinavian noir and rural Australian crime, among others. Yet the basic appeal Poe discovered remains. A crime throws the world out of balance, and the detective, by thinking hard and noticing what others miss, puts it right again."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Who created the detective C. Auguste Dupin?", ["Arthur Conan Doyle", "Edgar Allan Poe", "Wilkie Collins", "Agatha Christie"], 1, "Poe introduced Dupin in 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue' in 1841."],
    [2, "Who was a real life model for Sherlock Holmes?", ["Joseph Bell", "Charles Dickens", "Robert Peel", "Ronald Knox"], 0, "Doyle based Holmes partly on Joseph Bell, an Edinburgh medical teacher."],
    [3, "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab was set in which city?", ["London", "Sydney", "Melbourne", "Edinburgh"], 2, "Fergus Hume's 1886 bestseller was set in Melbourne."],
    [4, "Which detective was introduced in Christie's first novel?", ["Miss Marple", "Lord Peter Wimsey", "Philip Marlowe", "Hercule Poirot"], 3, "The Mysterious Affair at Styles (1920) introduced Poirot."],
    [5, "What was the main principle of Golden Age 'fair play'?", ["The detective must be a police officer", "Readers must be shown every clue", "The murderer must be a stranger", "Every story must be set in London"], 1, "Readers were meant to have the same clues as the detective."]
  ]);

WB.L("fairy-tale-origins", "literature", "beginner", "The dark origins of fairy tales", "Before Disney, Cinderella's stepsisters cut off parts of their feet to fit the slipper. Fairy tales have a long, strange and sometimes gruesome history.",
  [
    ["Stories older than writing", "Fairy tales began as spoken folk tales passed down for generations.", "Long before anyone wrote them down, fairy tales were told aloud: by grandparents at the fire, by workers passing the time, by nurses to children. They were not originally just for kids. Adults told stories of clever peasants, cruel stepmothers, talking animals and magical helpers to entertain each other and pass on lessons about the world. Because they lived in memory, they changed with each teller and travelled across borders. That is why very similar stories pop up in many countries. Folklorists have catalogued thousands of these tale types in a system now called the Aarne Thompson Uther index, which gives each story pattern a number. Cinderella type stories, for example, are found across Europe, Asia and Africa."],
    ["Cinderella around the world", "Versions of the Cinderella story have been recorded for well over a thousand years.", "The Greek geographer Strabo, writing around 2,000 years ago, recorded a tale about a woman named Rhodopis whose sandal was snatched by an eagle and dropped in the lap of an Egyptian king, who then searched for its owner. In China, a story written down in the 9th century tells of Ye Xian, a kind girl mistreated by her stepmother, who loses a golden shoe at a festival and is found by a king. Her magical helper is not a fairy godmother but the bones of a fish. These versions differ in detail, but they share a core: a mistreated young woman, a magical helper, a lost shoe and a royal search. The pattern clearly appealed to people everywhere."],
    ["Perrault's polished tales", "In 1697 a French writer turned folk tales into elegant stories with morals.", "In 1697, Charles Perrault, a member of the French Academy, published a small book of stories that included 'Cinderella', 'Sleeping Beauty', 'Little Red Riding Hood' and 'Puss in Boots'. He polished folk tales for fashionable readers at the court of Louis XIV, adding wit and ending each tale with a little moral in verse. Perrault gave us the pumpkin coach and the glass slipper. His Red Riding Hood ends badly: the wolf eats her, and there is no woodcutter to save her. The moral warns young women about smooth talking men. Earlier, the Italian writer Giambattista Basile had published his own collection of tales in the 1630s, including early versions of Cinderella and Sleeping Beauty."],
    ["The Brothers Grimm", "Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm collected German tales to preserve a national culture.", "In 1812, two German scholars, Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm, published the first volume of their Children's and Household Tales. They were language experts and wanted to preserve what they saw as the authentic voice of the German people, at a time when Germany was divided and occupied by Napoleon's France. Their image of simple peasants telling ancient tales is a little misleading. Many of their storytellers were educated middle class young women, some from families of French Huguenot descent, who knew Perrault's tales. One of their most valued sources, Dorothea Viehmann, was a tailor's widow with a vast memory for stories. The brothers revised the collection repeatedly, and the final edition of 1857 contained around 200 tales."],
    ["Softer, but bloodier", "The Grimms removed sexual content from later editions but kept plenty of violence.", "The first Grimm edition was not a children's book, and parents complained. So in later editions, Wilhelm in particular reworked the tales for family reading. Sexual hints were removed. In the first edition of 'Rapunzel', the witch discovers the prince's visits when Rapunzel innocently remarks that her clothes are getting tight around her waist. Later versions changed this to a more innocent slip of the tongue. Evil mothers were often turned into stepmothers, to protect the ideal of motherhood. Violence, however, mostly stayed, because it punished wickedness. In the Grimms' Cinderella, the stepsisters cut off a toe and a heel to fit the slipper, and at the wedding, birds peck out their eyes."],
    ["Andersen's invented tales", "Hans Christian Andersen wrote original fairy tales rather than collecting old ones.", "The Danish writer Hans Christian Andersen did something different. Rather than collecting folk tales, he mostly invented new ones, starting in 1835. 'The Little Mermaid', 'The Ugly Duckling', 'The Emperor's New Clothes' and 'The Snow Queen' are all his. Andersen's tales are often deeply melancholy. In his original 'Little Mermaid', published in 1837, the mermaid does not marry the prince. He marries someone else, and she gives up her chance at revenge, dissolves into sea foam and becomes a spirit of the air. Many readers see Andersen's own loneliness and feelings of being an outsider in his stories. The 'Snow Queen' later inspired, very loosely, Disney's Frozen, almost two centuries after it was written."],
    ["Why they still matter", "Fairy tales survive because they help us think about fear, hope and change.", "Fairy tales have been retold by Disney, rewritten by novelists such as Angela Carter, and analysed by psychologists, historians and feminists. In 1976 the psychologist Bruno Bettelheim argued that the frightening parts help children face their fears safely, though his ideas are now debated. Some researchers have used methods borrowed from biology to trace how tales spread between languages. A 2016 study suggested that a few stories, such as 'The Smith and the Devil', might be several thousand years old, though such estimates are uncertain. Whatever their age, fairy tales keep returning because they deal with big themes in simple shapes: growing up, leaving home, injustice, luck and courage. Each generation retells them to suit its own worries."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the Aarne Thompson Uther index?", ["A list of banned books", "A catalogue of folk tale types", "A history of German printing", "A ranking of bestselling children's books"], 1, "It classifies folk tales by their story patterns."],
    [1, "In the Chinese story of Ye Xian, what is her magical helper?", ["A fairy godmother", "A talking cat", "The bones of a fish", "A golden bird"], 2, "Ye Xian is helped by the magical bones of a fish."],
    [2, "Which detail did Charles Perrault add to Cinderella?", ["The glass slipper", "The birds pecking out eyes", "The fish bones", "The eagle"], 0, "Perrault gave us the glass slipper and pumpkin coach."],
    [4, "What did the Grimms tend to keep in later editions?", ["Sexual hints", "Evil birth mothers", "Violent punishments of wrongdoers", "Their original prefaces unchanged"], 2, "Violence that punished wickedness mostly stayed."],
    [5, "How does Andersen's original 'Little Mermaid' end?", ["She marries the prince", "She becomes a spirit of the air", "She returns to her father", "She becomes a human queen"], 1, "She dissolves into sea foam and becomes a spirit of the air."]
  ]);

WB.L("frankenstein", "literature", "intermediate", "Frankenstein and the birth of science fiction", "A teenager, a stormy summer and a ghost story contest produced one of the most influential novels ever written.",
  [
    ["The year without a summer", "Frankenstein was conceived during a strangely dark and stormy summer in 1816.", "In April 1815, Mount Tambora in what is now Indonesia exploded in one of the largest volcanic eruptions in recorded history. Its ash and gases spread around the globe and cooled the climate. In Europe, 1816 became known as the year without a summer, with cold, rain and failed harvests. That June, a group of young English writers was staying near Lake Geneva in Switzerland. The poet Lord Byron rented the Villa Diodati, and nearby stayed the poet Percy Bysshe Shelley, eighteen year old Mary Godwin, her stepsister Claire Clairmont, and Byron's doctor, John Polidori. Trapped indoors by the storms, they read ghost stories aloud, and Byron proposed that each of them write one."],
    ["A waking dream", "Mary Shelley later described the idea arriving in a vivid, half waking vision.", "Mary struggled for days to think of a story. Then, after listening to Byron and Percy discuss whether life could be artificially created, she had what she later described as a waking dream. She pictured a pale student kneeling beside a thing he had put together, watching it stir into life, and then fleeing in horror. That image became the heart of Frankenstein. She described this in an introduction to the revised 1831 edition, so it is her own account years after the event, but it has become one of the most famous origin stories in literature. Polidori's contribution to the contest led to The Vampyre, published in 1819, which helped shape the image of the vampire as an elegant aristocrat."],
    ["An extraordinary young author", "Mary Shelley came from a remarkable family and wrote the novel before she turned twenty one.", "Mary was the daughter of two famous radical thinkers. Her mother, Mary Wollstonecraft, wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, one of the founding works of feminism, and died shortly after Mary was born. Her father, William Godwin, was a political philosopher. Mary grew up surrounded by books and visiting writers. At sixteen she ran away with Percy Shelley, who was then married to someone else, causing a scandal. Her life was marked by tragedy, including the deaths of several of her children. Frankenstein was published anonymously in London in January 1818, when she was twenty. Many reviewers assumed the author was a man, and some guessed it was Percy Shelley himself."],
    ["Science at the edge of life", "The novel drew on real experiments with electricity and dead bodies.", "Frankenstein was not pure fantasy. In the 1780s, the Italian scientist Luigi Galvani had made the legs of dead frogs twitch using electricity, leading to the term galvanism. His nephew Giovanni Aldini went further. In London in 1803, he applied electric currents to the body of an executed criminal, making the face grimace and a hand rise, to the alarm of onlookers. Many people wondered whether electricity might be the spark of life itself. Mary Shelley knew about these debates. In the novel, Victor Frankenstein is deliberately vague about his methods, but the idea of a scientist using cutting edge knowledge to cross a forbidden line came straight out of the real science of her day."],
    ["The creature speaks", "In the novel, the creature is intelligent, articulate and deeply lonely.", "Anyone who knows Frankenstein only from films may be surprised by the book. First, Frankenstein is the scientist, Victor, not the creature, which is never given a name. Second, the creature is no grunting brute. Abandoned by his horrified creator, he teaches himself to speak and read by secretly watching a family through a gap in a cottage wall. He reads Milton's Paradise Lost and compares himself to both Adam and Satan. When he finally confronts Victor, he speaks with great eloquence about his loneliness and the cruelty he has suffered. He asks Victor to make him a companion. Victor's refusal sets off the tragic final chain of events. The real horror of the book is rejection."],
    ["The first science fiction novel?", "Many critics see Frankenstein as the starting point of science fiction.", "Earlier stories had featured magic and monsters, and Frankenstein grew out of the Gothic tradition of haunted castles and dark secrets that began with Horace Walpole's The Castle of Otranto in 1764. But Mary Shelley did something new. Her monster was created not by a curse or a spell but by a scientist using knowledge and method. The British writer Brian Aldiss argued that this makes Frankenstein the first true science fiction novel, a view many critics share. The subtitle, The Modern Prometheus, points to the ancient myth of the Titan who stole fire from the gods for humanity and was punished. Shelley's question was a new one: what responsibility do creators have for what they make?"],
    ["From page to pop culture", "Stage and film versions turned the creature into an icon, while changing him a lot.", "Within a few years of publication, Frankenstein was adapted for the London stage, and the story escaped its author's control. The most influential version came in 1931, when Universal Pictures released Frankenstein starring Boris Karloff. The make up artist Jack Pierce created the flat topped head and neck bolts that most people now picture. Karloff's creature barely speaks, so the eloquent figure of the novel was largely lost. Over the following century, Frankenstein became shorthand for any science that seems to go too far: genetically modified crops were nicknamed 'Frankenfoods', and debates about cloning and artificial intelligence still invoke it. The novel's warning is subtler than the slogan, though. Victor's real failure is abandoning what he made."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What caused the 'year without a summer' in 1816?", ["A solar eclipse", "The eruption of Mount Tambora", "An unusually long winter in Switzerland", "A comet passing Earth"], 1, "The 1815 Tambora eruption cooled the global climate."],
    [1, "What did John Polidori write after the ghost story contest?", ["The Vampyre", "Dracula", "The Castle of Otranto", "Paradise Lost"], 0, "Polidori's The Vampyre was published in 1819."],
    [3, "Giovanni Aldini became known for what?", ["Inventing the battery", "Applying electricity to an executed criminal's body", "Writing the first Gothic novel", "Discovering oxygen"], 1, "In 1803 he made a corpse's face and limbs move with electric current."],
    [4, "How does the creature in the novel learn to speak and read?", ["Victor teaches him", "He is born knowing", "By secretly watching a family", "From a schoolteacher"], 2, "He watches and listens to a family through a gap in a cottage wall."],
    [5, "What does the subtitle 'The Modern Prometheus' refer to?", ["A Roman emperor", "A Titan who stole fire for humanity", "A famous scientist", "An Egyptian god"], 1, "Prometheus stole fire from the gods and was punished."]
  ]);

WB.L("children-learning-language", "literature", "advanced", "How children learn to talk", "By around age four, most children have mastered the basics of a staggeringly complex grammar without a single lesson. How do they do it?",
  [
    ["Listening before birth", "Babies start tuning into language before they are even born.", "Language learning starts earlier than most people think. In the final months of pregnancy, a baby can hear sounds from outside the womb, muffled but with their rhythm and melody intact. Studies suggest newborns prefer their mother's voice, and even the rhythm of the language they heard in the womb, over unfamiliar ones. Researchers have found that newborns' cries may carry traces of the melody of their parents' language, with some research comparing French and German babies. In the first months, babies are also impressive generalists. They can tell apart speech sounds from almost any language in the world, including distinctions their parents cannot hear, such as sounds that are different in Hindi or Zulu but similar to English ears."],
    ["Narrowing down", "Around the end of the first year, babies tune out sounds their language does not use.", "That universal ability does not last. Research led by the Canadian psychologist Janet Werker and others found that by around 10 to 12 months, babies become much less sensitive to sound differences that are not used in the language around them. A baby raised in an English speaking home, for example, gradually stops noticing contrasts that matter in Hindi. This is not a loss so much as a specialisation. The brain is committing to the sounds that matter, which makes learning words much easier. It also helps explain why adults learning a new language often struggle with certain sounds, such as Japanese speakers distinguishing English 'r' and 'l', or English speakers hearing the tones of Mandarin."],
    ["From babbling to words", "Children follow a broadly similar path from babbling to sentences.", "Around six or seven months, many babies begin babbling, repeating syllables like 'bababa' or 'dadada'. Deaf babies exposed to sign language babble with their hands in a similar rhythm, which suggests babbling is about language, not just mouth practice. First words usually arrive around the first birthday, though the range is wide. For a while, words come slowly. Then, often somewhere in the second year, many children go through a vocabulary burst, adding new words at a rapid pace. Two word phrases like 'more milk' and 'daddy gone' appear, followed by longer sentences. By around four, most children speak in complex sentences. Every child is different, so these ages are general guides rather than strict deadlines."],
    ["Mistakes that prove learning", "Errors like 'goed' and 'mouses' show children are working out the rules.", "One of the most charming signs of language learning is also one of the most revealing. A child who once correctly said 'went' may suddenly start saying 'goed'. A child who said 'mice' may switch to 'mouses'. Parents sometimes worry, but this is actually progress. The child has discovered a rule, add '-ed' for the past or '-s' for plurals, and is applying it everywhere, even to exceptions. Linguists call this overregularisation. Nobody taught the child 'goed', and they almost certainly never heard an adult say it. They invented it from the pattern. Over time, children learn the exceptions again, and 'went' returns. The mistakes show that children are not just copying, they are building a system."],
    ["Born to speak?", "Scientists have long debated how much of language ability is built in.", "In the 1950s and 1960s the American linguist Noam Chomsky argued that children could not learn the complex rules of language just from the limited, messy speech they hear. He proposed that humans have an inborn capacity for language, sometimes called universal grammar, that shapes how all children learn. This idea dominated linguistics for decades. Other researchers, such as the psychologist Michael Tomasello, argue that children learn language through general thinking skills, such as spotting patterns and understanding what other people intend, combined with huge amounts of practice. Most scientists today agree that humans have some special readiness for language, but they still disagree about how much is built in and how much is learned. The debate remains lively."],
    ["A language invented by children", "Deaf children in Nicaragua created a brand new sign language.", "In the late 1970s and 1980s, new schools in Nicaragua brought together deaf children who had previously lived isolated from one another, often using only simple home signs with their families. The schools taught lip reading and Spanish, with little success. But in the playground and on buses, the children began signing to each other, and a shared language started to form. Remarkably, younger children who joined later made the language more systematic and complex than the older ones had. Linguists studied this new language, now called Nicaraguan Sign Language, as a rare chance to watch a language being born. It suggests that children do not just learn languages. Given the chance, they can create and refine them."],
    ["Helping a child's language grow", "Conversation, reading and play are among the best things adults can offer.", "Research suggests that what matters most for young children is not flashcards or apps but back and forth conversation. Responding to a baby's babbles, naming things during everyday routines, asking questions and waiting for answers all help. Reading together is especially valuable, because books contain words and sentence types that rarely come up in daily chat. Many people find the singsong voice adults use with babies, sometimes called parentese, a little silly, but studies suggest it can help babies pay attention to speech. Children raised with two or more languages are not confused by them, and bilingualism is common worldwide. This is general information. For milestones, see raisingchildren.net.au, and if you have concerns, talk to your GP, child health nurse or a speech pathologist."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "By around what age do babies become less sensitive to sound differences not used in their language?", ["2 to 3 months", "10 to 12 months", "3 years", "5 years"], 1, "Research by Janet Werker and others found this narrowing at around 10 to 12 months."],
    [2, "What does babbling in deaf babies exposed to sign language suggest?", ["Babbling is just mouth practice", "Deaf babies cannot learn language", "Babbling is about language, not only speech", "Sign language is learned later than speech"], 2, "Deaf babies babble with their hands, so babbling is a language milestone."],
    [3, "What does a child saying 'goed' instead of 'went' usually show?", ["A hearing problem", "They have learned a grammar rule", "They are copying adults", "They are falling behind"], 1, "It shows the child has discovered the '-ed' rule and is overapplying it."],
    [4, "Which linguist proposed an inborn 'universal grammar'?", ["Noam Chomsky", "Michael Tomasello", "Janet Werker", "Samuel Johnson"], 0, "Chomsky argued humans have an inborn capacity for language."],
    [5, "What was remarkable about Nicaraguan Sign Language?", ["It was designed by teachers", "It was copied from American Sign Language", "Deaf children created it themselves", "It has no grammar"], 2, "Deaf children in Nicaraguan schools created it, and younger children made it more complex."]
  ]);

WB.L("hero-journey-stories", "literature", "advanced", "The hidden shapes of stories", "Is there really only a handful of plots in the world? Writers and scientists have long tried to map the hidden structures beneath the stories we love.",
  [
    ["A story has a shape", "Since ancient times, thinkers have tried to describe how stories are built.", "Think of almost any film you have enjoyed. Someone wants something, obstacles get in the way, things get worse, and then there is a turning point and a resolution. That feels natural, but it is a pattern, and people have been trying to describe it for more than two thousand years. In his Poetics, written in the 4th century BCE, the Greek philosopher Aristotle observed that a good tragedy has a beginning, a middle and an end, and that its events should follow from one another by necessity or probability. That sounds obvious, but his point was that a plot is a chain of causes, not just a list of events. Writers still build stories around that insight today."],
    ["Freytag's pyramid", "A 19th century German critic drew drama as a rising and falling pyramid.", "In 1863 the German novelist and critic Gustav Freytag published an analysis of drama, especially ancient Greek plays and Shakespeare. He described their structure as a pyramid in five parts. There is an introduction, then rising action as conflict builds, then a climax at the peak, then falling action as consequences unfold, and finally a catastrophe or resolution. Many school students still learn a version of this as the story arc. Freytag's model fits five act tragedies like Hamlet quite well, but it is less helpful for many modern novels and films, where the biggest moment usually comes near the end rather than in the middle. Still, it gave readers a useful vocabulary for talking about how tension rises and falls."],
    ["The hero's journey", "Joseph Campbell argued that hero myths around the world share a common pattern.", "In 1949 the American scholar Joseph Campbell published The Hero with a Thousand Faces. Drawing on myths from many cultures, he argued that hero stories often follow a similar path, which he called the monomyth. A hero leaves the ordinary world after a call to adventure, often with help from a mentor, crosses into an unfamiliar world, faces trials and a great ordeal, gains some reward or insight, and returns home transformed. Campbell's ideas became enormously influential in Hollywood. George Lucas has said Campbell's work shaped Star Wars, and the pattern was later popularised among screenwriters as a practical template. Critics argue Campbell picked examples that fit and ignored many that do not, but the idea remains widely used."],
    ["Seven basic plots?", "Some writers claim all stories can be grouped into a small number of types.", "People love the idea that there are only a few stories in the world. In 2004 the British journalist Christopher Booker published The Seven Basic Plots, a long book arguing that stories fall into types such as overcoming the monster, rags to riches, the quest, voyage and return, comedy, tragedy and rebirth. Others have proposed different numbers, from three to more than thirty. These schemes can be fun and genuinely useful for spotting patterns: Jaws and the story of David and Goliath really are both about overcoming a monster. But critics point out that the categories are so broad that almost anything can be made to fit, which means they can hide as much as they reveal."],
    ["Stories by computer", "Researchers have used computers to chart the emotional ups and downs of thousands of stories.", "The American novelist Kurt Vonnegut gave a popular lecture in which he drew stories as lines on a graph, with good fortune going up and bad fortune going down. A story like Cinderella rises, falls and then soars. In 2016 a team of researchers in the United States tested a version of this idea with computers. They analysed the emotional words in more than 1,000 works of fiction from the Project Gutenberg library and tracked how the mood changed across each book. They reported that most stories followed one of six basic emotional arcs, such as rise, fall, fall then rise, and rise then fall. Like all such studies, its findings depend on its methods and are open to debate."],
    ["Stories from other traditions", "Not every storytelling tradition follows the Western conflict driven arc.", "Most of these models come from European and American traditions, and they tend to assume that stories are driven by conflict between a hero and an obstacle. Other traditions work differently. A classical structure from Chinese poetry and later Japanese and Korean storytelling, often called kishotenketsu in Japanese, has four parts: introduction, development, a twist or unexpected turn, and a conclusion that brings the parts together. The twist need not be a conflict at all, just a surprising new angle. Aboriginal storytelling traditions are often closely tied to land and place, and many stories are connected to particular sites and to responsibilities for Country, rather than following a single hero's arc. These differences remind us that the Western plot is one model among many."],
    ["Why structure matters", "Understanding story shapes helps readers notice craft and helps writers find their way.", "Knowing these patterns does not mean every story must follow them. Many great books play with structure: they start at the end, tell events out of order, or refuse a tidy resolution. But understanding the common shapes is useful. As a reader, it helps you notice why a story feels satisfying or why it drags. As a writer, it gives you questions to ask: what does the main character want, what stands in the way, and what changes by the end? Psychologists have also found that people tend to remember information better when it is told as a story with causes and consequences. That may be why the same basic shapes keep appearing, from campfire tales to streaming series. We seem to think in stories."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Aristotle emphasise about a good plot?", ["It should be in verse", "Events should follow from one another", "It must have a happy ending", "It must feature gods"], 1, "Aristotle saw plot as a chain of causes linked by necessity or probability."],
    [1, "In Freytag's pyramid, where does the climax sit?", ["At the very beginning", "At the peak in the middle", "After the resolution", "It has no climax"], 1, "Freytag placed the climax at the top of the pyramid, in the middle."],
    [2, "Which filmmaker said Joseph Campbell's work shaped his films?", ["Steven Spielberg", "George Lucas", "Alfred Hitchcock", "Peter Jackson"], 1, "George Lucas has said Campbell influenced Star Wars."],
    [4, "How many basic emotional arcs did the 2016 computer study report?", ["Three", "Six", "Twelve", "Thirty six"], 1, "The researchers reported six basic emotional arcs."],
    [5, "What is distinctive about the kishotenketsu structure?", ["It has no ending", "Its third part is a twist that need not involve conflict", "It always features a hero", "It is written backwards"], 1, "Its four parts include a twist that can be a new angle rather than a conflict."]
  ]);
